     "text": "Round 7's 4096-element blocks with 4 warps stay the best measured result so far. This round switches to 2048-element blocks with 4 warps to test finer work granularity, keeping the round-7 cache hints.\n\n**Rationale (estimates, not measured):**\n- **Shorter tail.** At about 6.5 TB/s, a 4096-element block stays resident for several microseconds, which is a large slice of the roughly 20 \u00b5s runtime. Halving the block roughly halves that, so the last partially filled wave should end sooner.\n- **Bandwidth should hold.** Each thread now issues 2 vector loads of 16 bytes per tensor. With up to 16 blocks resident per SM, that is about 128 KB of loads in flight per SM. That should still cover memory latency at full bandwidth.\n- **Cache hints unchanged.** Inputs are streamed once with `evict_first`. The output is stored with `evict_last` so its lines can stay in L2 instead of competing with input reads during the kernel.\n- **Correctness for any `n`.** The kernel is one launch with no extra passes. A bounds mask is used whenever `n` is not a multiple of the block size; the benchmark size divides evenly.\n\n```python title=\"impl_triton.py\"\nimport torch\nimport triton\nimport triton.language as tl\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Fixed configuration literals\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 128-thread CTAs, 16 fp16 per thread per tensor (2 x 16B vector accesses per\n# tensor per thread). Half the per-CTA work of the 4096-element variant, so\n# CTA lifetimes are shorter and the end-of-grid tail (when too few CTAs remain\n# per SM to keep enough bytes in flight) is finer-grained, while 16 resident\n# CTAs/SM still keep ~128 KB of loads in flight per SM. Inputs are streamed\n# once (evict_first: preferred L2 victims); the output is stored with\n# evict_last so its dirty lines stay resident in L2 instead of being picked as\n# victims while input reads are in flight.\n_BLOCK = 2048\n_NUM_WARPS = 4\n_NUM_STAGES = 1\n\n_CONFIG = {\n    \"BLOCK\": _BLOCK,\n    \"num_warps\": _NUM_WARPS,\n    \"num_stages\": _NUM_STAGES,\n    \"elems_per_thread\": _BLOCK // (_NUM_WARPS * 32),\n    \"load_eviction_policy\": \"evict_first\",\n    \"store_eviction_policy\": \"evict_last\",\n    \"store_cache_modifier\": \"default\",\n}\n\n\n@triton.jit\ndef _vector_add_kernel(x_ptr, y_ptr, out_ptr, n,\n                       BLOCK: tl.constexpr, EVEN: tl.constexpr):\n    pid = tl.program_id(0)\n    offs = pid * BLOCK + tl.arange(0, BLOCK)\n    offs = tl.max_contiguous(tl.multiple_of(offs, BLOCK), BLOCK)\n    if EVEN:\n        x = tl.load(x_ptr + offs, eviction_policy=\"evict_first\")\n        y = tl.load(y_ptr + offs, eviction_policy=\"evict_first\")\n        tl.store(out_ptr + offs, x + y, eviction_policy=\"evict_last\")\n    else:\n        mask = offs < n\n        x = tl.load(x_ptr + offs, mask=mask, other=0.0,\n                    eviction_policy=\"evict_first\")\n        y = tl.load(y_ptr + offs, mask=mask, other=0.0,\n                    eviction_policy=\"evict_first\")\n        tl.store(out_ptr + offs, x + y, mask=mask,\n                 eviction_policy=\"evict_last\")\n\n\ndef run(x, y):\n    x = x.contiguous()\n    y = y.contiguous()\n    out = torch.empty_like(x)\n    n = x.numel()\n    if n == 0:\n        return out\n    grid = (triton.cdiv(n, _BLOCK),)\n    even = (n % _BLOCK) == 0\n    _vector_add_kernel[grid](\n        x, y, out, n,\n        BLOCK=_BLOCK, EVEN=even,\n        num_warps=_NUM_WARPS, num_stages=_NUM_STAGES,\n    )\n    return out\n\n\ndef get_last_config() -> dict:\n    return dict(_CONFIG)\n```",
     "type": "text"
    }
